        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">A treadmill is an indoor appliance, and the manuals say so.</strong>
            The ones we checked tell owners not to use their treadmills outdoors, and one names covered
            patios. Rain, condensation, humidity, sun and outdoor electrics are all hard on a machine
            built for a living room, and damage from them may not be covered by the warranty.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you want the outdoor feeling, put the treadmill by a window or patio door, or wheel it
            out only on dry days into a GFCI-protected outdoor outlet. For the in-between spaces, our
            guide to keeping a
            <a href="/treadmill-in-garage/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">treadmill in a garage</a>
